Consider this C# code:

```csharp
string label = new string(new[] { 'm', 'a', 'p' });
string saved = label;
label += "!";
string other = new string(new[] { 'm', 'a', 'p' });
```

Explain which statements create new string objects and why `saved` still contains `"map"`. Then explain the results of `saved == other` and `object.ReferenceEquals(saved, other)`.
